Any process that exits, no matter how, initially becomes a zombie until its parent (or, if the parent has already exited, the process that adopts it—typically PID 1 or another designated subreaper) successfully calls wait() or waitpid() to collect its termination status. Only then does the kernel remove the final process table entry.

